Prime the CR4 shadow, so cr4_set_bits() computes from a real CR4.

cr4_set_bits() and its relatives never read CR4. They compute from
this_cpu_read(cpu_tlbstate.cr4), a shadow primed once by cr4_init_shadow()
inside x86_64_start_kernel(). A cooperative guest does not run that function --
it reloads CR3 and would discard the address space the host built for it -- so
the shadow is whatever the per-CPU page happened to hold, and it reads back as
all ones.

Every cr4_set_bits() therefore computes an impossible CR4, with bits set above
31 where the architecture defines none. The host refuses to write it, which is
correct and is the guard that keeps a guest from handing Windows a nonsense
control register. But the feature the caller was enabling quietly does not get
enabled, and the instruction that needed it faults later with nothing to
connect it back.

That has now cost two walls, both looking like the CPU refusing a legal
instruction for no reason:

    xsetbv    at fpu__init_cpu_xstate+0x73, after CR4.OSXSAVE
    rdfsbase  at __rdfsbase_inactive, after CR4.FSGSBASE

One read of the live register fixes the class. It goes at the top of
setup_arch, next to the console, because it has to precede the first
cr4_set_bits() and the earliest of those is in FPU and CPU setup, a long way
further on.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
@@ -55,6 +55,7 @@
 #include <asm/proto.h>
 #include <asm/realmode.h>
 #include <asm/thermal.h>
+#include <asm/tlbflush.h>	/* cr4_init_shadow */
 #include <asm/unwind.h>
 #include <asm/vsyscall.h>
 
@@ -941,6 +942,31 @@
